WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.269 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:03.269 --> 00:00:07.139
-Hello, everyone, and welcome
to Thanksgiving "Corrections."

00:00:07.139 --> 00:00:10.143
We're putting this out
a day early,

00:00:10.143 --> 00:00:13.879
because it's Thanksgiving,
and...

00:00:13.879 --> 00:00:15.781
if we're not thankful
for "Corrections,"

00:00:15.781 --> 00:00:17.517
what is there
to be thankful for?

00:00:21.654 --> 00:00:26.526
I mentioned Flamin' Hot Cheetos,
and I talked about the sauce.

00:00:26.526 --> 00:00:28.061
I was corrected.

00:00:28.061 --> 00:00:30.829
Flamin' Hot Cheetos
do not have a sauce on them.

00:00:30.829 --> 00:00:33.399
They have a dust on them.

00:00:33.399 --> 00:00:37.970
Which I should have remembered
from the famous Cheetos slogan,

00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:39.639
"Mmm.
That's good dust."

00:00:39.639 --> 00:00:45.611
[ Laughter ]

00:00:45.611 --> 00:00:50.516
I mispronounced the member
of a crew team

00:00:50.516 --> 00:00:53.686
that does the steering.

00:00:53.686 --> 00:00:57.824
I pronounced it "cock-swain,"
which is incorrect.

00:00:57.824 --> 00:00:59.158
It's "cock-sun."

00:00:59.158 --> 00:01:04.463
In my defense, I pronounced it
the way it's spelled.

00:01:04.463 --> 00:01:08.601
But, of course, that's not
good enough at old Oxford.

00:01:10.937 --> 00:01:14.974
Oh, "cock-swain" -- how quaint.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:14.974 --> 00:01:17.276
That's how they get you.

00:01:17.276 --> 00:01:18.778
At Oxford.

00:01:18.778 --> 00:01:19.946
'Cause then you say to them,

00:01:19.946 --> 00:01:21.580
"Well, how do you pronounce it?"
and they say,

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:24.284
"Oh, perhaps you'll buy
one of our dictionaries."

00:01:24.284 --> 00:01:27.153
[ Laughter ]

00:01:27.153 --> 00:01:30.857
I don't want to go to Oxford,
I don't want to go to Cambridge.

00:01:30.857 --> 00:01:33.258
You imagine
going to Cambridge?

00:01:33.258 --> 00:01:34.594
Spending every day
worried you were

00:01:34.594 --> 00:01:40.166
about to get recruited
to be a Russian spy.

00:01:40.166 --> 00:01:43.536
[ Laughter ]

00:01:43.536 --> 00:01:45.704
"We love the Cambridge Four,
don't we?"

00:01:45.704 --> 00:01:49.075
[ Laughter ]
We love 'em.

00:01:49.075 --> 00:01:52.312
Burgess, Blunt, Philby,
and Maclean.

00:01:52.312 --> 00:01:55.080
"Cambridge Four!

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:56.316
They added a fifth later.

00:01:56.316 --> 00:02:00.253
Cairncross --
didn't care for him."

00:02:00.253 --> 00:02:02.688
It is helpful that it is
pronounced "cock-sun"

00:02:02.688 --> 00:02:05.491
and not the way it's spelled,
because that's how they caught

00:02:05.491 --> 00:02:08.695
a lot of the kids
in that admissions scandal.

00:02:08.695 --> 00:02:10.496
They would bring them in
at, say, Yale or Dartmouth,

00:02:10.496 --> 00:02:12.898
and they'd say,
"Oh, I see here you did crew."

00:02:12.898 --> 00:02:15.101
They'd say, "Yeah, I did crew."

00:02:15.101 --> 00:02:16.836
And they'd say,
"Now, I'm just wondering,

00:02:16.836 --> 00:02:18.604
did you have someone helping you
with the steering wheel,

00:02:18.604 --> 00:02:20.806
with your backs to the action
while you're rowing?"

00:02:20.806 --> 00:02:23.075
"Yeah. Yeah, we did."

00:02:23.075 --> 00:02:24.610
And they'd say,
"Just out of curiosity,

00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:26.379
what did you call that person?"

00:02:26.379 --> 00:02:29.449
"Uh, well, you know,
we'd call them Fred."

00:02:29.449 --> 00:02:33.319
[ Laughter ]

00:02:33.319 --> 00:02:37.223
"F-Fred did m-most
of the steering."

00:02:37.223 --> 00:02:38.558
"So, yeah, but did Fred
have a job?

00:02:38.558 --> 00:02:40.726
Like, what would you call --

00:02:40.726 --> 00:02:45.431
what would call Fred's role
in the crew team?"

00:02:45.431 --> 00:02:49.535
They'd go, "Uh...

00:02:49.535 --> 00:02:51.104
..."cock-swain"?
-"Get out!"

00:02:51.104 --> 00:02:57.410
[ Laughter ]

00:02:57.410 --> 00:03:00.613
We showed a graphic
of Joe Flacco --

00:03:00.613 --> 00:03:02.982
Jets quarterback Joe Flacco --

00:03:02.982 --> 00:03:06.485
wearing the number 5.
He's the number 19.

00:03:06.485 --> 00:03:09.388
In our defense, Joe Flacco
was on the Jets last year.

00:03:09.388 --> 00:03:11.591
He left the Jets
as a free agent.

00:03:11.591 --> 00:03:14.026
He was then traded
back to the Jets.

00:03:14.026 --> 00:03:17.763
He was 5 last year.
He's 19 this year.

00:03:17.763 --> 00:03:20.032
So, slightly defensible,

00:03:20.032 --> 00:03:21.834
but we heard
from a lot of Jets fans,

00:03:21.834 --> 00:03:25.471
because if there is one thing

00:03:25.471 --> 00:03:29.742
Jets fans demand,
it is error-free execution.

00:03:29.742 --> 00:03:34.047
[ Laughter ]

00:03:34.047 --> 00:03:38.051
We did --
The reason we had a joke

00:03:38.051 --> 00:03:40.686
about Joe Flacco
is he admitted --

00:03:40.686 --> 00:03:44.390
to his credit, he admitted
he was not vaccinated

00:03:44.390 --> 00:03:47.293
against COVID Five --
COVID-19 -- did it again.

00:03:47.293 --> 00:03:51.864
[ Laughter ]

00:03:51.864 --> 00:03:54.133
And I'm wondering, do you think
he would have got vaccinated

00:03:54.133 --> 00:03:56.102
if the vaccine protected you

00:03:56.102 --> 00:03:58.037
from being traded
back to the Jets?

00:03:58.037 --> 00:04:02.241
[ Laughter ]

00:04:02.241 --> 00:04:04.510
If it like 95% prevented you

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:07.046
from being traded back
to the Jets.

00:04:07.046 --> 00:04:08.247
I feel like, at that point,

00:04:08.247 --> 00:04:11.317
you'd stop doing
your own research.

00:04:11.317 --> 00:04:14.954
"I got COVID twice."
"You think you got it bad?"

00:04:14.954 --> 00:04:17.990
[ Laughter ]

00:04:17.990 --> 00:04:20.760
This is embarrassing.

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:24.163
We implied...

00:04:24.163 --> 00:04:28.267
that you send Beetlejuice away
by saying his name three times,

00:04:28.267 --> 00:04:30.203
but. of course, you summon

00:04:30.203 --> 00:04:32.805
Beetlejuice by saying his name
three times.

00:04:32.805 --> 00:04:37.876
I don't -- I do not know
if this will work, but, um...

00:04:37.876 --> 00:04:42.381
Beetlejuice, Beetlejuice,
Beetlejuice.

00:04:42.381 --> 00:04:45.418
[ FaceTime ringing ]

00:04:53.826 --> 00:04:57.863
Nope.

00:04:57.863 --> 00:05:01.334
As a consolation prize,

00:05:01.334 --> 00:05:04.537
this is my impression of me
asking Michael Keaton...

00:05:04.537 --> 00:05:07.140
[ Ringtone plays ]

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:08.573
[ Laughter ]

00:05:08.573 --> 00:05:10.208
Someone's calling me.

00:05:10.208 --> 00:05:14.313
[ Ringtone continues ]

00:05:14.313 --> 00:05:17.049
[ FaceTime call ends ]
Oh.

00:05:17.049 --> 00:05:18.151
[ FaceTime ringing ]

00:05:18.151 --> 00:05:23.556
[ Laughter ]

00:05:23.556 --> 00:05:25.925
[ FaceTime call starts ]
-Hey. What's up?

00:05:25.925 --> 00:05:29.162
Yeah, do, uh --
do Beetlejuice.

00:05:29.162 --> 00:05:32.431
-"Uh, you hate sandworms?
Can't stand 'em myself."

00:05:32.431 --> 00:05:33.432
[ Croaks ]

00:05:33.432 --> 00:05:36.702
-[ Laughs ]
Love you.

00:05:36.702 --> 00:05:46.212
[ Laughter ]

00:05:46.212 --> 00:05:48.548
Worth every one
of the three phone calls.

00:05:48.548 --> 00:05:51.217
[ Laughter ]

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:58.357
Um -- Oh, so we've made
a few jokes about --

00:05:58.357 --> 00:05:59.826
It was in the news.

00:05:59.826 --> 00:06:03.629
It was reported
in a lot of the news that,

00:06:03.629 --> 00:06:06.965
in a new comic, Superman...

00:06:06.965 --> 00:06:10.002
would come out --
was gonna come out as bisexual.

00:06:10.002 --> 00:06:12.705
Now, I should clarify,
and a lot of people

00:06:12.705 --> 00:06:14.240
are very happy
with the number of

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:17.242
comic-book fans
who watch this show.

00:06:17.242 --> 00:06:20.947
It's not Clark Kent Superman
who came out as bisexual.

00:06:20.947 --> 00:06:22.215
It's his son, Jonathan Kent,

00:06:22.215 --> 00:06:25.184
who sometimes serves
the role of Superman.

00:06:25.184 --> 00:06:27.219
And they've been
the most lovely comments,

00:06:27.219 --> 00:06:30.890
because it is comic-book fans
who care very much

00:06:30.890 --> 00:06:33.058
about getting the details right,
who also want to make it clear

00:06:33.058 --> 00:06:35.361
they're not upset about
the sexuality part of it.

00:06:35.361 --> 00:06:38.831
So, like, "It's not Superman!
That would be fine."

00:06:38.831 --> 00:06:41.100
[ Laughter ]

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:45.070
But it's Jonathan Kent
who is bisexual, and, you know,

00:06:45.070 --> 00:06:50.209
Clark Kent,
the Superman we know, um...

00:06:50.209 --> 00:06:51.844
I think his sexuality
is very clear.

00:06:51.844 --> 00:06:54.846
He doesn't he doesn't go back
and forth, right?

00:06:54.846 --> 00:06:57.116
He stays in his lane.

00:06:57.116 --> 00:07:00.319
[ Laughter ]

00:07:09.228 --> 00:07:12.131
I like that joke so much
that I, um...

00:07:12.131 --> 00:07:15.400
I reached out and asked
if I can get a Superman T-shirt

00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:17.636
because I was going to think
about ripping this open

00:07:17.636 --> 00:07:19.238
and having it.

00:07:19.238 --> 00:07:21.841
And I mentioned to Eric --

00:07:21.841 --> 00:07:26.445
incredibly talented man who
runs our wardrobe department --

00:07:26.445 --> 00:07:29.215
and he said,
"You want to do what?"

00:07:29.215 --> 00:07:38.424
[ Laughter ]

00:07:38.424 --> 00:07:39.792
Mentioned baby carrots.

00:07:39.792 --> 00:07:42.194
I got some very interesting
feedback on baby carrots.

00:07:42.194 --> 00:07:46.298
Two kinds of baby carrots.
There are baby carrots you grow.

00:07:46.298 --> 00:07:48.467
But there are also
baby-cut carrots.

00:07:48.467 --> 00:07:50.035
These are carrots, I think,

00:07:50.035 --> 00:07:53.005
that are more common
in a supermarket.

00:07:53.005 --> 00:07:57.142
These are cut
from regular-sized carrots

00:07:57.142 --> 00:07:59.545
and have far less
nutritional value

00:07:59.545 --> 00:08:05.885
than baby-grown carrots
versus baby-cut carrots and...

00:08:05.885 --> 00:08:08.120
someone -- a jackal posited
that they think that

00:08:08.120 --> 00:08:09.755
the baby-cut carrots
come from carrots

00:08:09.755 --> 00:08:12.324
that are so misshapen
that people wouldn't buy them.

00:08:12.324 --> 00:08:13.658
So that's how they're making --

00:08:13.658 --> 00:08:15.794
they're still getting
their carrot money out of it.

00:08:15.794 --> 00:08:17.730
But you have to be careful
if you ever --

00:08:17.730 --> 00:08:19.030
and I'm not recommending
you do this.

00:08:19.030 --> 00:08:21.366
If you ever buy
your baby carrots

00:08:21.366 --> 00:08:25.637
on the black market,
just know that

00:08:25.637 --> 00:08:29.208
your connect, if you will,

00:08:29.208 --> 00:08:31.677
might do a thing,
which is very common

00:08:31.677 --> 00:08:33.679
in this kind of deal,
where they'll show up

00:08:33.679 --> 00:08:35.047
with a duffel bag
or, you know,

00:08:35.047 --> 00:08:36.883
some of the fancier guys
still use briefcases,

00:08:36.883 --> 00:08:40.319
and what they'll do is, they'll
show you the baby carrots,

00:08:40.319 --> 00:08:43.089
and they'll put the good
baby carrots up top.

00:08:43.089 --> 00:08:47.293
But then underneath,
it's just baby-cut carrots.

00:08:47.293 --> 00:08:49.028
So you just have to --
just know, reach --

00:08:49.028 --> 00:08:50.930
before you pay the money
for your baby carrots,

00:08:50.930 --> 00:08:54.033
you know, dig in the bottom.

00:08:54.033 --> 00:08:56.402
You know, it's --
I use a jeweler's loop.

00:08:56.402 --> 00:08:59.538
[ Laughter ]

00:08:59.538 --> 00:09:00.606
You look for a cut.

00:09:00.606 --> 00:09:01.907
And then if you still
can't tell --

00:09:01.907 --> 00:09:05.277
This is weird, and
I know this sounds like a bit.

00:09:05.277 --> 00:09:08.748
The way you can tell is,
you go like this.

00:09:08.748 --> 00:09:10.750
[ Laughter ]

00:09:14.286 --> 00:09:15.721
And then if you feel like
you've been ripped off,

00:09:15.721 --> 00:09:17.122
it's very important to just look

00:09:17.122 --> 00:09:18.991
and, you know,
look at your dealer

00:09:18.991 --> 00:09:21.227
and say something
along the lines of like,

00:09:21.227 --> 00:09:23.396
"Do you take me for a fool?"

00:09:23.396 --> 00:09:25.197
[ Laughter ]

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:29.501
Corn.

00:09:29.501 --> 00:09:33.038
I said corn was a vegetable.
A lot of feedback on that.

00:09:33.038 --> 00:09:34.907
Not -- I still don't have
any clarity.

00:09:34.907 --> 00:09:37.009
USDA says
it's a starchy vegetable.

00:09:37.009 --> 00:09:38.544
Some of you said it's a grain.

00:09:38.544 --> 00:09:41.747
Some of you said it's a grain
when you take it off the cob.

00:09:41.747 --> 00:09:44.783
It's a vegetable
when you have it on the cob.

00:09:44.783 --> 00:09:47.786
But it did, you know --
led me to my research, which --

00:09:47.786 --> 00:09:50.488
You know, sometimes
the best part about Corrections

00:09:50.488 --> 00:09:55.061
is that I'm learning new things
to impart upon you.

00:09:55.061 --> 00:09:56.862
This is a true story about corn.

00:09:56.862 --> 00:10:01.033
The first Thanksgiving,
the Pilgrims, 1621,

00:10:01.033 --> 00:10:03.536
they invited
the Wampanoag tribe,

00:10:03.536 --> 00:10:07.206
and they said to the leader,

00:10:07.206 --> 00:10:10.475
"We would like to have you
for Thanksgiving."

00:10:10.475 --> 00:10:12.945
And the head of the Wampanoag
tribe said, "Great.

00:10:12.945 --> 00:10:14.146
Is there anything we can bring?"

00:10:14.146 --> 00:10:15.814
And they said,
"Sure. A vegetable."

00:10:15.814 --> 00:10:18.083
So then it's Thanksgiving Day,

00:10:18.083 --> 00:10:21.887
and the Wampanoag tribe
shows up with corn.

00:10:21.887 --> 00:10:26.491
And one of the Pilgrims,
a woman --

00:10:26.491 --> 00:10:28.494
I don't want
to be critical of her.

00:10:28.494 --> 00:10:32.264
The Pilgrims famously had
a very hard first year --

00:10:32.264 --> 00:10:36.134
rough winter, new land,

00:10:36.134 --> 00:10:40.205
left all their friends behind,
long boat ride.

00:10:40.205 --> 00:10:46.411
So there are reasons
that they would maybe be rude.

00:10:46.411 --> 00:10:49.547
But what happened is the
Wampanoag showed up and said,

00:10:49.547 --> 00:10:51.883
"Here's our vegetable,"
and they brought their corn,

00:10:51.883 --> 00:10:56.455
and this female Pilgrim, like,
mumbled and her breath

00:10:56.455 --> 00:10:59.191
and said,
"If ya call corn a vegetable."

00:10:59.191 --> 00:11:00.960
[ Laughter ]

00:11:09.001 --> 00:11:11.536
And that was, um --

00:11:11.536 --> 00:11:13.706
That was the first Jacksgiving.

00:11:13.706 --> 00:11:15.474
[ Laughter ]

00:11:19.044 --> 00:11:21.547
I said some things wrong.

00:11:21.547 --> 00:11:24.316
Couple of flubs.

00:11:24.316 --> 00:11:29.988
I said Ray-Mans glasses
instead of Ray-Bans.

00:11:29.988 --> 00:11:33.359
I said "skate bark"
instead of "skate park."

00:11:33.359 --> 00:11:35.928
And I said "Traders Joe's."

00:11:35.928 --> 00:11:38.164
Which I think sounds better.

00:11:43.268 --> 00:11:45.904
We say -- Someone said,
"You say this a lot

00:11:45.904 --> 00:11:47.105
and you're getting it wrong."

00:11:47.105 --> 00:11:49.908
Semblance of normalcy.

00:11:49.908 --> 00:11:53.212
And I will -- that does sound
like something Sal would write.

00:11:55.814 --> 00:11:58.050
Technically,
the correct vocabulary

00:11:58.050 --> 00:12:01.220
is "semblance of normality,"
and this is fascinating.

00:12:01.220 --> 00:12:03.122
Warren Harding,
President Warren Harding

00:12:03.122 --> 00:12:06.525
in the '20s gave a speech
where he had a malaprop,

00:12:06.525 --> 00:12:10.061
and he accidentally said
"semblance of normalcy,"

00:12:10.061 --> 00:12:12.864
and then that became acceptable,
even though it's not --

00:12:12.864 --> 00:12:14.899
was not a word
before he said it wrong,

00:12:14.899 --> 00:12:17.970
and that just goes to show you
you can make a mistake

00:12:17.970 --> 00:12:19.904
that then becomes accepted.

00:12:19.904 --> 00:12:22.040
A hundred years from now,

00:12:22.040 --> 00:12:25.811
we might all call it
Traders Joe's.

00:12:25.811 --> 00:12:27.913
[ Laughter ]

00:12:30.850 --> 00:12:35.054
I am now very excited to...

00:12:35.054 --> 00:12:38.123
give what I think is the best
Correction I will ever give.

00:12:38.123 --> 00:12:40.025
I feel very confident
this is the best Correction

00:12:40.025 --> 00:12:41.326
I will ever give.

00:12:41.326 --> 00:12:44.697
I have said on this show
many times

00:12:44.697 --> 00:12:46.865
that I am the father of two.

00:12:46.865 --> 00:12:48.967
That is incorrect.

00:12:48.967 --> 00:12:54.874
For the last 10 weeks,
I have been the father of three.

00:12:54.874 --> 00:13:00.979
10 weeks ago, my wife gave
birth to a beautiful baby girl.

00:13:00.979 --> 00:13:04.383
Usually, this is something
I would announce on the show,

00:13:04.383 --> 00:13:06.585
but we all decided

00:13:06.585 --> 00:13:09.822
that Corrections
could use the traffic.

00:13:09.822 --> 00:13:11.857
[ Laughter ]

00:13:17.029 --> 00:13:20.766
Her name is --
And I should say you --

00:13:20.766 --> 00:13:23.269
We knew what we were going
to name her for a long time.

00:13:23.269 --> 00:13:24.636
And you have to be careful.

00:13:24.636 --> 00:13:26.538
I think everyone who's ever
had a kid knows this.

00:13:26.538 --> 00:13:27.872
You want to be careful

00:13:27.872 --> 00:13:29.374
telling people the name
before a baby is born

00:13:29.374 --> 00:13:31.009
because some people
in your family

00:13:31.009 --> 00:13:33.378
might go so far as to tell you
why they don't like it,

00:13:33.378 --> 00:13:35.447
and that ruins it for you,
so you have to wait.

00:13:35.447 --> 00:13:37.115
You wait until the baby's born,

00:13:37.115 --> 00:13:39.485
and then you say what the name
is because it's too late

00:13:39.485 --> 00:13:41.386
at that point
for anybody to say anything.

00:13:41.386 --> 00:13:44.089
However, some of us --
some of us have people

00:13:44.089 --> 00:13:47.592
in our families
who will still comment on a name

00:13:47.592 --> 00:13:50.629
after it's too late
to change it.

00:13:50.629 --> 00:13:56.701
I have a sister-in-law.
Love her very much.

00:13:56.701 --> 00:13:58.503
Dear, dear friend of mine.

00:13:58.503 --> 00:14:02.107
Knew her before I knew my wife.

00:14:02.107 --> 00:14:04.043
Her name escapes me.

00:14:04.043 --> 00:14:05.578
[ Laughter ]

00:14:08.881 --> 00:14:10.382
But my sister-in-law --
And let me say

00:14:10.382 --> 00:14:12.918
if you don't have -- if you
don't have a sister-in-law,

00:14:12.918 --> 00:14:16.889
get -- go out
and get one posthaste.

00:14:16.889 --> 00:14:18.257
They are -- It's the --

00:14:18.257 --> 00:14:20.926
It's like your wife
with none of the intimacy

00:14:20.926 --> 00:14:22.628
and all of the opinions.

00:14:22.628 --> 00:14:24.630
[ Laughter ]

00:14:27.866 --> 00:14:32.904
So, we had our first son.

00:14:32.904 --> 00:14:37.342
We named him Ashe,
which is my wife's maiden name.

00:14:37.342 --> 00:14:39.812
And his middle name is Olson,

00:14:39.812 --> 00:14:41.513
which is my mother's
maiden name.

00:14:41.513 --> 00:14:44.382
And we thought
it was a beautiful tribute

00:14:44.382 --> 00:14:46.618
to two incredible women.

00:14:46.618 --> 00:14:48.220
And my sister-in-law,

00:14:48.220 --> 00:14:53.158
when she heard that we named
our son Ashe Olson Meyers, said,

00:14:53.158 --> 00:14:55.727
"Are you worried people
will think you named him

00:14:55.727 --> 00:14:58.430
after Ashley Olsen?"

00:14:58.430 --> 00:14:59.865
And I immediately said,

00:14:59.865 --> 00:15:02.835
"I don't think a single person
will think that."

00:15:05.070 --> 00:15:06.905
They might think
it sounds like Ashley Ols--

00:15:06.905 --> 00:15:08.706
I don't think a single person
will be like,

00:15:08.706 --> 00:15:11.276
"Well, you know Seth.

00:15:11.276 --> 00:15:15.613
If there's anyone he was
going to pay tribute to..."

00:15:15.613 --> 00:15:17.516
I mean, I've never even --
I've never even seen

00:15:17.516 --> 00:15:19.818
an episode of "Full House."

00:15:19.818 --> 00:15:22.054
Name a kid...

00:15:22.054 --> 00:15:24.523
Lovely person.
I've met her. Lovely.

00:15:24.523 --> 00:15:28.093
But name a kid after
Ashley Olsen? I mean...

00:15:28.093 --> 00:15:32.931
[ Laughter ]

00:15:32.931 --> 00:15:37.235
So...that's the risk.

00:15:37.235 --> 00:15:39.405
Anyway, I'm sorry.
I completely --

00:15:39.405 --> 00:15:40.906
Sometimes I lo-- All right.

00:15:40.906 --> 00:15:43.675
So, the baby's name.
We named her Mary Kate.

00:15:43.675 --> 00:15:45.744
[ Laughter ]

00:15:56.755 --> 00:15:59.391
If you want to hear her name,

00:15:59.391 --> 00:16:04.363
if you want to see pictures,
if you want to hear the story,

00:16:04.363 --> 00:16:06.264
you have to watch
the Thanksgiving episode,

00:16:06.264 --> 00:16:07.465
which is on tonight.

00:16:07.465 --> 00:16:09.667
My parents are on,
and this is something

00:16:09.667 --> 00:16:11.402
that we're just
gonna start doing more.

00:16:11.402 --> 00:16:13.338
We're gonna use this show
to promote Corrections,

00:16:13.338 --> 00:16:15.074
Corrections to promote the show.

00:16:15.074 --> 00:16:19.044
Shoemaker, you've always said
your favorite word -- "synergy."

00:16:19.044 --> 00:16:20.879
[ Laughter ]

00:16:23.582 --> 00:16:25.484
This was a joke
I decided not to do

00:16:25.484 --> 00:16:27.385
because it tonally
didn't seem right

00:16:27.385 --> 00:16:29.521
based on the emotion
of having a new kid.

00:16:29.521 --> 00:16:30.923
I was gonna say,

00:16:30.923 --> 00:16:33.925
"Do you guys want to see
a picture of my baby?"

00:16:33.925 --> 00:16:36.361
Again, I'm not gonna do this
'cause, tonally, it's wrong.

00:16:36.361 --> 00:16:38.664
Say, "You guys want to see
a picture of my new baby?"

00:16:38.664 --> 00:16:40.398
And then I'd show this,

00:16:40.398 --> 00:16:43.535
and then I'd go, "Which
one of you was with her?!"

00:16:43.535 --> 00:16:45.771
[ Laughter ]

00:16:54.679 --> 00:16:58.316
It's Thanksgiving, and...

00:16:58.316 --> 00:17:01.019
I am thankful for a lot.

00:17:01.019 --> 00:17:05.290
Many things are new
about my life

00:17:05.290 --> 00:17:08.394
that weren't true last year.

00:17:08.394 --> 00:17:10.696
Beautiful baby girl
is one of them.

00:17:10.696 --> 00:17:16.201
Another one -- less important
but also lovely --

00:17:16.201 --> 00:17:18.303
is the advent of Corrections.

00:17:18.303 --> 00:17:23.975
And I just want to say
doing this every week

00:17:23.975 --> 00:17:25.677
makes my life
a little bit better.

00:17:25.677 --> 00:17:27.946
And I couldn't do it
without you.

00:17:27.946 --> 00:17:32.150
So as much as I hate
to say this,

00:17:32.150 --> 00:17:34.920 line:0%
I want to share that...

00:17:34.920 --> 00:17:39.891 line:0%
I am thankful for every one
of you that takes the time

00:17:39.891 --> 00:17:43.295 line:0%
to leave a comment
on our YouTube page.

00:17:48.100 --> 00:17:53.572 line:0%
We are off for a week.
See me in two.

